course: dictionary
lesson: 14792
title: Pulpy: squashy or cheap?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have two meanings, and the sentence tells you which one to use. Pulpy can mean soft and squashy, like very ripe fruit. It can also describe a cheap, exciting story that is not very well written.
explain: «The peach was so ripe that it was pulpy and sweet.» Ripe fruit that is pulpy feels soft and mushy when you press it. The clues are ripe, sweet and peach, so pulpy means soft and squashy here.
explain: «The detective story was pulpy, with wild car chases and no real clues.» Here the clues are the story, the chases and no real clues. A story can be pulpy when it is exciting but not well made, so the second meaning fits.
fact: Pulp is the soft, wet inside of fruit.
fact: Pulpy means soft, wet and squashy, like pulp.
fact: «Pulp» magazines were cheap, exciting stories, so a pulpy story is one that is exciting but not very well written.
fact: Wood can be made into pulp to make paper.
fact: When a word has two meanings, read the words around it to choose the right one.
exercise: «The peach was ripe and pulpy, and its juice ran down my chin.» What does pulpy mean? | Hard and dry | *Soft and squashy | Loud and noisy | Cheap and exciting
exercise: «The comic was pulpy: wild chases, no real characters, and it was not well written.» What does pulpy mean here? | Soft and wet | *Cheap, exciting and not very well written | Full of juice | Clean and tidy
exercise: Which word in «The orange juice was thick and pulpy, with bits floating in it.» tells you the juice has soft bits in it? | thick | orange | floating | *pulpy
test: «The bread fell in the puddle and became pulpy.» What does pulpy mean here? | Clean and dry | *Soft, wet and squashy | Full of loud noise | Crispy and golden
test: Which sentence uses pulpy to mean cheap, exciting and badly written? | The wood was pulpy and ready for the fire | *The thriller was pulpy, full of silly chases and no real plot | The bread was pulpy and warm | The banana was pulpy and brown
exam: Which meaning fits «The pulpy novel had car chases on every page»? | *Cheap, exciting and not very well written | Soft and squashy | Full of pulp for making paper | Wet from the rain
